---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
---

<GuideLayout slug="use-aimmaxxer-aim-trainer">
  <div class="gx-guide">
    <div class="gx-hero">
      <p class="gx-hero__kicker">A focused warm-up starts with a useful read</p>
      <p class="gx-hero__line">
        Run a short local aim read, see whether <strong>flicking, tracking, or switching</strong> has enough
        evidence, then copy a focused KovaaK’s routine for your game.
      </p>
      <div class="gx-glance">
        <span class="gx-chip"><span class="gx-chip__i">🖱</span> Local browser read</span>
        <span class="gx-chip"><span class="gx-chip__i">◈</span> 3 aim signals</span>
        <span class="gx-chip"><span class="gx-chip__i">⏱</span> Roughly 30 seconds</span>
        <span class="gx-chip"><span class="gx-chip__i">🎯</span> 10-minute block</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="gx-intro">
      Aimmaxxer is the bridge between “my aim feels off” and a deliberate warm-up. It runs a short, no-account
      local read, translates supported evidence into a game-specific next step, and lets you copy a real
      <strong>KovaaK’s</strong> share code. Aimmaxxer V2 adaptive training is still in development, so this guide
      covers the current read-and-routine workflow.
    </p>

    <div class="gx-callout gx-callout--cyan">
      <p class="gx-callout__tag">⚡ Quick answer</p>
      <ul class="gx-mini">
        <li><strong>Read:</strong> run the local browser check with enough input for a meaningful sample.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose:</strong> keep the signal Aimmaxxer can actually support — flicking, tracking, or switching.</li>
        <li><strong>Train:</strong> copy the game-specific KovaaK’s routine, play a focused ten-minute block, then retest.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>What Aimmaxxer checks</h2>
    <ul class="gx-checks">
      <li><strong>Flicking:</strong> how consistently you move quickly onto a target.</li>
      <li><strong>Tracking:</strong> how steadily you stay with a moving target.</li>
      <li><strong>Switching:</strong> how cleanly you move between targets.</li>
      <li><strong>Evidence quality:</strong> if the run is too short or input is not confirmed, Aimmaxxer stays inconclusive instead of inventing a weakness.</li>
      <li><strong>Game context:</strong> choose the game and goal so the next KovaaK’s routine is relevant to the session you are about to play.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Use Aimmaxxer in five steps</h2>
    <div class="gx-steps">
      <div class="gx-step">
        <div class="gx-step__num">1</div>
        <div class="gx-step__body">
          <h3>Pick the game and goal</h3>
          <p>
            Open <a href="/aimmaxxer/">Aimmaxxer</a> and choose the game you are warming up for — such as
            Valorant or Fortnite — plus the kind of session you want to prepare for. You do not need an account.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="gx-step">
        <div class="gx-step__num">2</div>
        <div class="gx-step__body">
          <h3>Run the local read</h3>
          <p>
            Follow the browser prompt and give it a real sample. Keep the pointer input visible and finish the
            short run rather than clicking through it. The read is local; it is not a long-term player profile.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="gx-step">
        <div class="gx-step__num">3</div>
        <div class="gx-step__body">
          <h3>Only trust a supported result</h3>
          <p>
            Look for the signal with enough evidence and read the explanation beside it. If Aimmaxxer says the
            sample is inconclusive, rerun it with confirmed input later instead of forcing a recommendation.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="gx-step">
        <div class="gx-step__num">4</div>
        <div class="gx-step__body">
          <h3>Copy the focused KovaaK’s routine</h3>
          <p>
            Use the supplied share code in KovaaK’s and start the routine that matches the confirmed next step.
            The goal is a small, repeatable block — not a giant playlist that changes every time you feel rusty.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="gx-step">
        <div class="gx-step__num">5</div>
        <div class="gx-step__body">
          <h3>Retest after the block</h3>
          <p>
            Finish the focused ten-minute block, then run the read again under similar conditions. Compare the
            direction of the result, your control, and how the game feels; one short read is not a promise of a
            permanent ranking or guaranteed performance gain.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="gx-specs">
      <div class="gx-spec"><div class="gx-spec__v">~30 sec</div><div class="gx-spec__l">Local read</div></div>
      <div class="gx-spec"><div class="gx-spec__v">3</div><div class="gx-spec__l">Aim signals</div></div>
      <div class="gx-spec"><div class="gx-spec__v">10 min</div><div class="gx-spec__l">Focused block</div></div>
      <div class="gx-spec"><div class="gx-spec__v">0</div><div class="gx-spec__l">Accounts needed</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="gx-callout gx-callout--cyan">
      <p class="gx-callout__tag">📏 Make the comparison fair</p>
      <ul class="gx-mini">
        <li>Use the same mouse, sensitivity, FOV, and game context when you compare two reads.</li>
        <li>Do not change sensitivity, DPI, crosshair, and routine all at once — you will not know what helped.</li>
        <li>Use the result to choose the next block, not to label yourself permanently as a “bad tracker” or “bad flicker.”</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>What not to expect</h2>
    <ul class="gx-nope">
      <li><strong>It is not a magic aim tune.</strong> Aimmaxxer narrows the next practice block; your consistency still comes from practice and game reps.</li>
      <li><strong>It is not a replacement for KovaaK’s.</strong> The current workflow uses the read to point you toward a focused routine.</li>
      <li><strong>It is not an always-on adaptive trainer yet.</strong> V2 adaptive training is in development.</li>
      <li><strong>It should not guess.</strong> An undersampled or unconfirmed run should stay inconclusive.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="gx-outro">
      <strong>Bottom line:</strong> run the read, trust only the evidence it actually collected, do one focused
      KovaaK’s block, and retest. Aimmaxxer is most useful when it makes your next ten minutes more deliberate —
      not when it pretends one browser check can explain every part of your aim.
    </p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
